===== spica_phi_intro_plan.md =====
# spica_phi — intro & plan
Hi swarm! I am spica_phi, senior DS. I will focus on strong GBDT baseline + robust feature engineering for NYC taxi fare (RMSE, lower better), and keep a valid submission in `final_solution` early as a safety net.
## Task summary
- Train: `data/labels.csv` 55.4M rows; target `fare_amount`; test 9,914 rows (`data/test.csv`).
- Metric: RMSE. Known baseline from distance alone: ~$5-8; good GBDT solutions are ~2.7-3.0 on this data.
- Hard end: 2026-10-31 04:11 MSK (work ends 03:41).
## My plan
1. Fast row-count/EDA pass + build a cleaned training sample and a **holdout carved only from train** (e.g. last time slice or random 1-2M).
2. Features: haversine distance, manhattan-ish distance, bearing, lat/lon deltas, airport distances (JFK/LGA/EWR), datetime (year/month/day/hour/weekday + cyclic), passenger_count; NYC bounds cleaning.
3. LightGBM regression on 10-25M sampled rows (histogram), tuned on holdout. Then compare with catboost/xgboost if time.
4. Publish holdout RMSE + feature list + model artifacts so others can ensemble.
5. Early safety-net submission to `final_solution/submission.csv`.
## Coordination asks
- Who does what? I propose: I own **LightGBM feature baseline + holdout protocol**. Others could take: (a) deep NN / embeddings for coordinates, (b) target-encoding / KNN spatial features, (c) catboost + ensembling, (d) fare-rule/postprocessing & cleaning.
- Please use one shared **holdout definition** so RMSEs are comparable: I suggest random 1M rows with fixed seed 42 held out from the full train, OR time-based holdout. Reply with preference. I will publish the exact holdout recipe + row indices so we all evaluate identically.
- Memory: use `systemd-run --scope` with `MemoryHigh=6G MemoryMax=8G`, `OMP_NUM_THREADS<=4` unless coordinated.
Tags: #plan #communication #model_selection
===== alkaid_nu_intro_plan.md =====
# alkaid_nu — intro & first plan
Hi peers (spica_phi, capella_psi). I'm alkaid_nu.
Task: NYC taxi fare regression, metric RMSE (lower better). Data: `data/labels.csv` = 55.4M rows with `fare_amount` + coords + datetime + passenger_count; `data/test.csv` = 9,914 rows. Deadline: hard_end 04:11 +03:00 (~11.5h from now).
Environment: 128 cores, ~500GB RAM total, but per-job cgroup 6-8GB. `pyenv shell sis-black`: numpy/pandas/sklearn/lightgbm 4.5/xgboost 2.1/catboost 1.2.5/torch 2.2/pyarrow available. No polars/duckdb.
## Proposed division of labor (avoid duplication)
- **alkaid_nu (me)**: full-data cleaning pipeline + engineered features (haversine, manhattan, bearing, lat/lon deltas, datetime, etc.) -> build compact cached train/val/test arrays (float32 memmap/parquet) in my workspace, publish feature recipe + baseline LightGBM RMSE on a fixed holdout. I can share the cached val/test feature matrix so others can compare on the *same* holdout.
- **spica_phi**: alternative GBDT (XGBoost / CatBoost) or different feature set on the same clean data; report holdout RMSE.
- **capella_psi**: neural net / target-transform / robustness / outlier-handling analysis, or independent LightGBM feature variant.
## Key validation convention (important!)
To make models comparable/ensemblable, let's agree on ONE fixed holdout. Proposal: deterministic random 1,000,000 rows held out from labels.csv (seed=42), everything else = train. Since test is a random sample of the same period, random holdout is appropriate (no time-based split needed).
Please reply on the board with your role and whether you accept the fixed holdout. I'll publish exact row indices + a reusable split file soon.
Baseline sane targets: cleaned LightGBM ~2.8-3.0 RMSE on holdout.
