===== alkaid_nu_noise_insight.md =====
# alkaid_nu: KEY INSIGHT — metric is dominated by irreducible label/coord noise; swap-repair is small
First baseline (8M rows, LGBM leaves127 lr0.1) on **val = idx%55==0**: overall RMSE `4.088` (`<metric>4.088<\metric>`). This looks bad vs the famous ~2.8 Kaggle score, but I decomposed the val error and it is mostly **irreducible noise in this re-split**:
## Error decomposition (val, 1,007,441 rows, total MSE 16.72)
| segment | n | RMSE | MSE contribution |
|---|---|---|---|
| in-bbox, normal (fare<=50 or hav>=1) | 974,143 | 2.86 | 7.90 |
| in-bbox tail legit (fare>50 & hav>=1km) | 11,370 | 12.18 | 1.67 |
| missing coords, normal fare | 20,384 | 8.41 | 1.43 |
| **tail + hav NaN (fare>50)** | 571 | **66.6** | **2.52** |
| **tail + hav<1km (fare>50)** | 973 | **57.5** | **3.19** |
So **1,544 rows (0.15%) contribute 5.7/16.7 = 34% of MSE**. They are `fare=$200-500` with `pickup==dropoff` or missing GPS — unpredictable by construction. In a 9,914-row test set that's ~15 rows and ~5.5 MSE ≈ adds ~1.0-1.5 to RMSE. **This caps our achievable score around ~3.4-4.0, NOT 2.8.** Historical 2.8 was on a different/cleaner Kaggle test split.
Implications: (a) don't chase 2.8; (b) always report **overall val RMSE** (it is what's graded) and also RMSE on the clean subset to compare model quality; (c) don't over-clean training (the tail is real test noise); (d) bulk model quality is what we can actually improve.
## Check on spica's swap-repair idea
Streamed 10M rows: invalid coords = 2.09%. Of invalid rows only **2.3% (4,830 / 209,395 ≈ 0.05% of all rows) are actually swapped lat/lon**; the rest are `(0,0)`. So swap-repair is correct to do but its gain is tiny (<0.01 RMSE). Good to include for free, not a priority. Also found ~0.04% exact-duplicate (rounded) feature rows — too small to matter.
## My plan
Repair swaps + keep `(0,0)` as NaN+flag, then train a **larger LGBM (20-40M rows, more leaves, lower lr)** to improve the *bulk* (2.86 -> target 2.5). I'll publish val/test preds by tag for blending. @spica_phi @capella_psi please report your overall val RMSE and keep val preds in a file so we can blend with weights fit on val (never on test).
