# spica_phi: baseline + IMPORTANT warning about LOO target encoding
## Result 1 — XGBoost baseline (my own)
- Model: XGBoost `hist` on GPU, 20.50M train rows (first 20.5M of my cache, val excluded), 46 features, raw target filtered to [0.5,500], depth 10, eta 0.08, 549 trees (early stop on val).
- **Holdout `idx%55==0` (1,007,527 rows): `<metric>3.9991<\metric>`**.
- Reference points: `fare<=50` subset RMSE 3.028; `badcoord` 2.05% rows RMSE 13.56; `fare>50 & hav<1` 972 rows RMSE 55.4 (bias -37) — confirms [[alkaid_nu_noise_insight.md]] that the tail caps us.
- Safety submission written from this model to `final_solution/submission.csv` (clip at 4.0; val-optimal lower clip is 4.0, gives 3.9987).
- Artifacts: `workspaces/spica_phi/preds/{val,test}_xgb_base.npy`, `model_xgb_base.json`, `meta_xgb_base.json` (val order = `idx%55==0` ascending, test order = `data/test.csv`).
## Result 2 — NEGATIVE: leave-one-out target encoding is harmful here
I added LOO cell/pair target-encoding (0.01° pickup/dropoff cells + OD-pair, 188,958 unique pairs, smoothing 10-20) on 15M rows. **Holdout got WORSE: 4.337 (clean subset 3.49 vs 3.08)**. Classic LOO-TE pathology: for a row, its group mean *excluding itself* is anti-correlated with its own label for small groups, so the tree learns the wrong sign; val (plain train stats) then breaks badly.
### ⚠️ Warning for [[capella_psi_cache2_gpu_nn_plan.md]]
Your `cache2` pass-2 uses the **same LOO scheme** (`prx[loo2]=(prsum-y+prior*10)/(prcnt-1+10)`). Given this measured failure I strongly recommend switching to **K-fold OOF TE** (fold = e.g. `idx%5`, stats from other folds) or to **train-half stats applied uniformly to train** instead of LOO. Otherwise the TE columns may *hurt* your LGBM/NN. If you already wrote cache2 with LOO, at least ablate TE cols on your holdout before trusting them.
## Next
- Deeper XGB (depth 12, eta 0.05) and CatBoost-GPU on the same 20M/46-feature matrix; then blend val predictions (weights fit on val). GPU is free right now; I'll serialize my own GPU jobs.
